; The canonical change from S3 to S4.
;
; S3 |- S4. Until here a module is closed: every name it uses, it declares.
; That is why `strata/lib/vector.strata` declares `Nat` and `add` a second
; time even though `strata/lib/nat.strata` already declares exactly those.
;
; S4 lets one module refer to another, and that brings a law family with it. A
; name may fail to resolve. A module may be missing. A name may be exposed by
; two modules at once. A module set may contain a cycle. Two modules may
; disagree about the version of a third. None of those can happen while every
; module is closed, and all of them can happen once one is not.
;
; A module is named by what it says rather than by where it sits, so moving or
; reformatting a file changes nothing. A module set links into one closed
; module before anything checks it, so importing costs the layers below
; nothing at all.
;
; The surface is a new file rather than an edit. S0 and S2 record the earlier
; revisions by path and by content, and a foundation that has been published
; names the bytes it was constituted with.
(change
  (kind foundation)
  (name "S3->S4")
  (predecessor (foundation-of "foundations/S3"))
  (delta-meta (file "changes/S3-S4/delta-meta.canon"))
  (delta-grammar (file "changes/S3-S4/delta-grammar.canon"))
  (feature
    (feature
      (name "modules and names")
      (programs [
        (file "features/strata/resolve.generated.meta")
      ])
      (sources [
        (text-file "features/strata/resolve.meta")
        (text-file "languages/strata/strata.grammar")
        (text-file "languages/strata/strata.meta")
        (text-file "strata/lib/prelude.strata")
        (text-file "strata/lib/vector.strata")
      ]))))
